  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Widespread frost is expected for northern areas\ntonight, with patchy frost elsewhere in interior Downeast.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure makes its way into the forecast area today, with\nthe center of the 1030mb high crossing the northern half of the\nCWA overnight tonight. This will lead to clear skies and calm\nwinds, especially directly under the high, which will be prime\nconditions for efficient radiational cooling and temperatures\ndropping below freezing, especially in the north directly under\nthe high. Downeast will likely sit just south of the center of\nthe high, with a weak but non-zero pressure gradient leading to\npotentially continued light winds through the night, which will\never so slightly disrupt the effectiveness of the nocturnal\nradiational cooling. That said, temperatures may dip low enough\nfor a frost threat in the Bangor and Downeast regions.\n\nThe frost/freeze program has ended for the entire CWA aside for\nHancock and Washington counties. That said, there is still a\nchance for frost, particularly in northern Hancock and central \nWashington, especially should the center of the high pressure\nshift slightly south from where global models currently put it.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nSunday night, a cold surface high pressure that will have \nbrought widespread frost Sunday AM shifts to the east over the \nMaritimes. A potent vertically stacked low pressure system \ntracks into north-central Quebec with a trailing potent cold \nfront. That front will swing across the CWA on Monday at the \nsame time as a low pressure exits off the Mid-Atlantic coast. \nThe interaction between the high pressure and the two lows \nprovides significant uncertainty about rainfall. There continues\nto be a few different scenarios still with the models. 1. The \nhigh pressure could dominate, forcing the lows to stay to the \nnorth and south, limiting precip. 2. The Canadian low could \nenhance the coastal low as it rides closer to the coast \nnorthward, bringing more substantial precip to coastal areas. 3.\nThe Canadian low could dominate, bringing enhanced \nprecipitation along its cold front from the moisture over the \nsoutheast low. At this time, uncertainty from the models \ncontinues to be high, so forecast confidence is low, with a wide\nrange of precipitation values. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Strong westerly winds on Tuesday could lead to\nsome isolated power outages.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\nIncreasing confidence on Tuesday that, as the low pressure \nsystem deepens in the Northern Maritimes, we can expect strong \nsurface high pressure to dive over the eastern Great Lakes. This\nwill lead to a strong pressure gradient between the low and \nhigh resulting in gusty west winds developing during the \nafternoon and evening hours on Monday. Tuesday probabilities for\n35+ mph wind gusts have increased to 70-90% with 40+ mph \nprobabilities now 50-70% for Millinocket and areas north. For \nthe Bangor region and interior Downeast, 35+ mph wind gusts \nprobabilities are 50-70% with 40+ mph probabilities now 20-40%. \nThere is ensemble guidance suggesting a potential of wind \nadvisory headlines (31-39 mph sustained or gusts 45+ mph) but \nstill uncertainty exists on exact expected wind speeds/gusts. \nHowever, it is likely that significant leaf loss may be possible\nin areas of Northern Maine where peak foliage has been reached.\nAdditional leaf loss is possible in the Central Highlands to \nDowneast Maine where the foliage is moderate to high. Gusty \nwinds could also lead to some isolated power outages. Continue \nto monitor the forecast for updates, as models get a better \nhandle on the atmospheric set up.",
